Tar: tolerate binary xattr values in pax extended headers (macOS bsdtar)

macOS `tar` (bsdtar) stores extended attributes raw inside pax records --
notably the auto-applied, non-removable com.apple.provenance xattr, whose
value contains NUL bytes. Upstream 7-Zip treats any NUL inside a pax
payload as corruption: ReadDataToBuffer truncates StringSize at the first
NUL, the pax branch sets Pax_Error, that sets _is_Warning, and
TarHandlerOut.cpp UpdateItems() then returns E_NOTIMPL -- making every
macOS-created tar (and therefore .tgz) read-only in NextZip.

Fix: carry the true payload size (CTempBuffer::DataSize) alongside the
NUL-truncated StringSize, flag Pax_Error only on genuine truncation, and
have ParsePax skip just the NUL-bearing record (records are
"len key=value\n", so skipping by the declared length keeps the following
records parseable). Unmodified entries raw-copy their original headers on
update, so their xattr records survive; only a replaced entry loses its
xattr record (its content changed anyway).

Applied by deps/build-7z-engine.sh after extracting the upstream source.
Written against 7-Zip 26.01. NOTE: upstream sources are CRLF; this patch
preserves CRLF in its context lines -- do not run it through tools that
normalize line endings.

